Quarto seems to be running and active and even previews the file correctly creating the html preview file. The caveat is that I'm trying to use markdown instead of `.qmd`. However, even in `.qmd` it tries to run the code through slime. If I remove slime plugin, it throws an error with `slime#send` function not found.


I'm almost positive I'm missing something trivial, but I have tried everything including basing of the configuration in quarto-nvim-kickstart. All of the quarto-nvim configuration was extracted from molten. I can run some Molten commands fine, like MoltenRevaluateCell and some others. Sometimes Molten commands fail saying it's not in a Molten cell, but I believe that's more related to Molten than this. But for whatever command I run with Quarto, it always fallback to slime and since I don't have an attached terminal it simply prints `Terminal not found`

It would be nice if this plugin would support integration with [overseer.nvim](https://github.com/stevearc/overseer.nvim). Here is a simple template that works for me:

```lua
return {
  name = "quarto preview",
  builder = function()
    local file = vim.fn.expand("%:p")
    return {
      cmd = { "quarto" },
      args = { "preview", file },
      components = {
        "default",
      },
    }
  end,
  condition = {
    filetype = { "quarto" },
  },
}
```

You can of course do something similar for rendering etc.

But it would be nice to have this be aware of `_quarto.yml` like the implementation here is.

Tree sitter provides shortcuts to select meaningful chunks of text. For example, the `@function.outer` capture group selects the function (including definition) in which the cursor is contained. These capture groups can be mapped to keys (traditionally `af` for `@function.outer`).

The capture groups do not seem to work in `.qmd` files.

To reproduce, using the following standard treesitter neovim setup:

```
